  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nOklahoma and north Texas will remain in the more seasonable\nweather pattern through the remainder of the week. Through the\nmid-week, precipitation chances will mainly be confined to the\nwest where upslope post-frontal air assists in the development of\nconvection on the High Plains, but the question will be if these\nthunderstorms can move far enough east to reach the 100th\nMeridian. Storm chances increase Wednesday night and Thursday as\nmodels generally show a upper trough approaching the area,\nalthough there are differences in the models on the specific\nevolution of the pattern on Thursday/Friday which would potential\naffect timing and how high the precipitation chances will be late \nin the week.\n\nFor mid-week (Monday-Wednesday), there's about a 80% probability \nthat thunderstorms remain to our west, with a 20% probability of\nthe storms making their way into far western Oklahoma. \n\nFor Wednesday through Friday, overall, the rain and thunderstorm\nchances increase as the upper level storm moves closer. The rain \nchances appear to be maximized late Wednesday into Thursday, \nbringing the potential for widespread showers and thunderstorms. \nThis chance remains about 60%, with about a 40% chance of a \nslower, drying system moving into Oklahoma and western north \nTexas.\n\n26/21",
